{parent=3a}

$s(t)=\int^t|\dot x(u)|du$, $t=dx/ds$, $\kappa=|dt/ds|$, and $n=\kappa^{-1}dt/ds$. Differentiating $t\cdot t=1$ proves $t\perp n$. Here $|\dot x|=2e^t$ and
$$t=\tfrac12(\cos t-\sin t,\sin t+\cos t,\sqrt2),\quad n=\frac{(-2\sin t,2\cos t,\sqrt2)}{\sqrt{10}},\quad \kappa=\frac{\sqrt{10}}{4e^t}.$$

See also: <Curvature and torsion>.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= 4A
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Vector Calculus}

= Solution
{parent=4a}

<Divergence theorem> says $\int_V\nabla\cdot F\,dV=\int_{\partial V}F\cdot n\,dS$ for the outward normal. Here $\nabla\cdot(xe^{-r^2})=(3-2r^2)e^{-r^2}$, so radial integration gives $4\pi a^3e^{-a^2}$. On the sphere $F\cdot n=ae^{-a^2}$, whose integral is the same $4\pi a^3e^{-a^2}$.

See also: <Divergence theorem>.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= 5D
{parent=Paper 3}
