= a
{parent=5d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The order of $g$ is the least positive $r$ with $g^r=e$. Division $n=qr+s$ shows $g^n=e$ forces $s=0$. <Lagrange theorem> follows because left cosets of a subgroup partition $G$ into equal blocks; applying it to $\langle g\rangle$ shows $|g|\mid|G|$. Coset multiplication makes $G/N$ a group when $N\triangleleft G$. If $x\in N$, then $x^n=e$. Conversely, if $x^n=e$, the order of $xN$ divides both $m$ and $n$, hence is one, so $x\in N$.

See also: <Lagrange theorem>.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= b
{parent=5d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Every $b^m$ lies in $N$ because $(bN)^m=N$. Conversely choose $r$ with $mr\equiv1\pmod n$; for $a\in N$, $a=(a^r)^m$ because $a^n=e$.

See also: <Quotient group>.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

