{parent=6d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

If $x^2\equiv a$ and $y^2\equiv a\pmod p$, then $(x-y)(x+y)\equiv0$, hence $y\equiv\pm x$; these differ for odd $p$. It fails for composite moduli: $1$ has four square roots modulo $8$.

See also: <Quadratic residue>.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= iii
{parent=6d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For prime $n$, pair each nonzero residue with its inverse; only $\pm1$ are self-inverse, yielding <Wilson theorem>. Conversely, if $(n-1)!\equiv-1\pmod n$, every $1\le a\lt n$ is a unit (otherwise a common divisor would divide the left side and $n$ but not $-1$), so $n$ is prime.

See also: <Wilson theorem>.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

